To address these limitations, this paper presents the complete engineering design, implementation, and empirical performance characterization of an embodied, multilingual, and multimodal conversational agent deployed on the \textbf{UNIHIKER K10} development board (DFRobot DFR0992-EN). The platform is powered by an Espressif ESP32-S3 dual-core system-on-chip (SoC) and executes the open-source \textbf{xiaozhi-esp32} firmware ecosystem, interfacing directly with DeepSeek foundation models via full-duplex WebSocket conduits. Crucially, the system introduces native support for \textbf{both English and Hindi spoken interactions}, real-time camera-based visual question answering (VQA), and Model Context Protocol (MCP) voice-actuated hardware control.

\subsection{Research Questions}
This investigation is structured around three central research questions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ$_1$ (Latency Decomposition):} Under the compute and memory constraints of an ESP32-S3 microcontroller, what is the achievable end-to-end round-trip latency of a voice conversational turn, and how is this latency apportioned across edge VAD, network transport, cloud STT, DeepSeek LLM inference, and neural TTS synthesis?
    \item \textbf{RQ$_2$ (Multilingual Parity):} How does bilingual conversational operation (English versus Hindi) impact recognition accuracy (Word Error Rate vs. Character Error Rate), token generation latency, and synthesis naturalness across different acoustic noise environments?
    \item \textbf{RQ$_3$ (Multimodal and MCP Overhead):} What is the computational, memory, and transmission overhead incurred when streaming camera frames for visual Q\&A and executing Model Context Protocol (MCP) hardware commands relative to baseline audio-only conversational turns?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Key Contributions}
The primary contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We design and deploy a complete edge-to-cloud multilingual conversational AI architecture on the UNIHIKER K10 (ESP32-S3) platform, combining open-source \textit{xiaozhi-esp32} firmware with the DeepSeek language reasoning backend.
    \item We engineer a bilingual voice pipeline supporting natural English and Hindi dialogue, Devanagari acoustic transcription, dynamic language identification, and expressive dual-language neural TTS playback.
    \item We implement multimodal visual perception via the onboard camera for live scene analysis (``Take a photo for me'', ``What are you looking at?'') with display rendering, coupled with Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool execution for voice-driven peripheral actuation.
    \item We formulate formal execution-loop algorithms governing the multi-threaded edge pipeline, network state transitions, and tool-calling execution.
    \item We present comprehensive empirical benchmark data detailing latency breakdowns, microcontroller resource footprints (SRAM, PSRAM, Flash, CPU load, power dissipation), and acoustic noise robustness (35~dB to 70~dB SPL).
\end{enumerate}

