\section{Literature Review and Related Work}

\subsection{TinyML and Edge-Cloud Hybrid Computing}
TinyML has emerged as a critical domain focusing on running machine learning inference on devices consuming under 1~mW to several watts of power \cite{ray2022tinyml}. While early TinyML implementations focused primarily on static keyword spotting, sensor anomaly detection, and basic image classification, the explosion of foundation models has exposed the fundamental ceiling of microcontrollers: an ESP32-S3 cannot store or compute the billions of floating-point weights required for contextual natural language understanding. Consequently, hybrid architectures have gained prominence. In such systems, the edge node acts as an intelligent sensory filter, executing voice activity detection (VAD), audio compression (e.g., Opus), and sensor fusion locally, while offloading high-dimensional matrix operations to the cloud. This aligns with the communication paradigms analyzed by Jain \emph{et al.} \cite{jain2026lightweight}, who demonstrated that resource-constrained IoT nodes can maintain secure, high-integrity communication channels by structuring payloads efficiently and minimizing uncompressed raw data transmission.

\subsection{Speech Recognition and Multilingual Acoustic Modeling}
Machine-learning-based voice processing underpins the conversational front end. In their comprehensive survey, Mahmood and Abdulazeez \cite{mahmood2024voice} evaluated classification algorithms including Support Vector Machines (SVM), Multilayer Perceptrons (MLP), and Random Forests (RF) across audio feature representations such as Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs). While traditional embedded systems rely on closed-set speech classifiers, conversational assistants require continuous, open-vocabulary Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR).

Deploying speech recognition for Indo-Aryan languages like Hindi presents unique acoustic and orthographic challenges. Hindi phonology includes retroflex consonants, aspirated plosives, and nasalized vowels that differ substantially from English phonetic structures \cite{hanifa2021review, kabir2021speaker}. Modern cloud-based ASR engines such as Whisper-large-v3 and FunASR mitigate these challenges through deep cross-attention transformer models trained on multilingual speech datasets. However, streaming Hindi audio from a microcontroller requires robust edge packetization to preserve phoneme boundaries during network transport.

