\subsection{Multimodal Vision and Model Context Protocol (MCP) Actuation}
In addition to voice dialogue, the system integrates two advanced embodied capabilities:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Voice-Triggered Visual Recognition:} When the user issues a visual inquiry (e.g., ``Take a photo for me'', ``What are you looking at?'', or Hindi equivalents such as ``Dekho aur batao ye kya hai''), the cloud orchestrator detects the visual intent and sends a capture control signal back to the K10 over WebSocket. The K10 pauses audio streaming, activates the GC0308 camera, captures a QVGA (320$\times$240) JPEG image frame, renders the captured snapshot directly on the 2.8-inch LCD for user confirmation, and uploads the frame to the cloud gateway. The image is fused into a Vision-Language Model (VLM) context alongside the user's prompt, and the resulting semantic description is spoken back via TTS.
    \item \textbf{MCP-Mediated Hardware Actuation:} The platform implements the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to allow DeepSeek to actuate physical hardware. The system registers peripheral tool definitions (JSON schema) with the model. When a user requests a hardware state change (e.g., ``Turn all lights blue'', ``Set the first light yellow'', or ``Light ko laal kar do''), DeepSeek outputs a structured tool-call payload:
    \begin{verbatim}
    {"tool": "set_rgb", "params": {"index": 0, "r": 0, "g": 0, "b": 255}}
    \end{verbatim}
    The Xiaozhi gateway forwards this JSON command to the K10 over the downstream WebSocket channel. The firmware parses the payload and commands the WS2812 driver via RMT pulses, providing instant physical confirmation of the voice command.
\end{itemize}

