\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\end{table*}

The empirical findings in Table~\ref{tab:latency_breakdown} reveal that DeepSeek language reasoning is the largest individual latency contributor, accounting for 39.2\% to 41.0\% of the overall turn time, followed by Cloud STT (21.3\%--22.7\%) and Neural TTS (19.8\%--21.8\%). Edge buffering and network transport collectively account for less than 18\% of the total latency budget. 

Regarding language parity (\textbf{RQ$_2$}), Hindi conversational turns exhibit a modest latency overhead of 9.1\% (1617.7~ms versus 1482.3~ms). This delta stems from two factors: (1) higher acoustic complexity in Devanagari phoneme alignment during STT processing (+32.6~ms on average), and (2) slightly higher tokenization lengths generated by sub-word tokenizers for non-Latin Devanagari scripts during LLM inference and TTS phoneme generation (+41.7~ms). Despite this variance, both languages remain well beneath the 2000~ms conversational threshold, providing a highly fluid, human-perceptible dialogue cadence.

