As documented in Table~\ref{tab:mcp_benchmarks}, MCP peripheral tool-calling commands achieve a 98.5\% execution success rate with a total response latency of under 525~ms, providing near-instantaneous physical responses to spoken commands. Visual recognition exhibits a 94.0\% accuracy rate with a total turnaround time of 1580~ms, confirming that camera-based perception can be seamlessly fused into conversational turns without causing perceptible lag.

% ============================================================
% SECTION VI: CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This paper designed, implemented, and empirically characterized an edge-assisted, multilingual conversational AI terminal built on the UNIHIKER K10 single-board computer (ESP32-S3) running the open-source \textit{xiaozhi-esp32} firmware paired with the DeepSeek language reasoning model. By establishing full-duplex WebSocket audio streaming, on-device VAD, and cloud-coordinated STT and neural TTS, the system demonstrates the practical viability of delivering human-like voice interaction on low-power microcontroller hardware. Crucially, the platform bridges a vital linguistic divide by natively supporting both English and Hindi conversations with seamless code-switching, while incorporating real-time camera-based visual Q\&A and Model Context Protocol (MCP) physical actuator control of onboard RGB LEDs.

Comprehensive empirical evaluations show that the system achieves average conversational round-trip latencies of 1482~ms for English and 1618~ms for Hindi, while maintaining peak internal SRAM consumption below 60.2\% and total active power dissipation under 1.45~W. These findings substantiate that microcontroller-class edge nodes can serve as embodied, multilingual AI companions in educational, smart-home, and assistive robotics contexts.

Future work will focus on:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{On-Device Small Language Models (SLMs):} Exploring 2-bit and 4-bit quantized micro-transformers (such as MobileLLM or TinyLlama) deployed on the ESP32-S3 PSRAM to handle basic conversational queries offline, reverting to DeepSeek only for complex inquiries.
    \item \textbf{Acoustic Front-End Enhancements:} Implementing neural noise suppression (RNNoise) and dual-microphone beamforming on Core~1 to enhance Hindi phonetic clarity in environments exceeding 70~dB SPL.
    \item \textbf{Lightweight Cryptographic Payloads:} Incorporating lightweight payload encryption ciphers (e.g., Enhanced TEA \cite{jain2026lightweight} or ASCON \cite{dworkin2023ascon}) to guarantee privacy for user voice transcripts and biometric visual streams over public networks.
\end{enumerate}

